# SquirrelJME Font Resource Specification (`.sqf`)

SquirrelJME Fonts, or `.sqf`, are bitmap fonts that are able to read and used
by ScritchUI to display glyphs on screen. This is intended to be a memory
efficient format with optional compression.

## Glyph Order

All glyphs follow the ISO/IEC 10646, Universal Coded Character Set, order.
This also means that the first 256 glyphs are in ISO/IEC 8859-1 order.

## Compression

Compression is done with the Raster Font Compression for Embedded Systems
(RaFoCES) algorithm. This is done via Vertex Chain Codes within a Huffman
table. Effectively, a trace is done around font glyphs and a directional
vector is chosen to follow which produced a chain of vectors accordingly.

Compression is optional and individual glyphs may or may not be compressed,
if they are compressed then they share the same compression data.

Observationally, RaFoCES can decrease the amount of space a font takes up
however it is observed that general Deflate compression offers the same
space-saving benefit to RaFoCES in general scenarios with similarly paired
glyphs. Currently, SquirrelJME takes a naive approach due to SQF limitations
to keep memory usage down so this does not provide a bonus to storage space.

Algorithm reference:

 * <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.patcog.2010.07.018>

